data: {"type":"tool_execution_start","toolCallId":"toolu_01Q2QifZFzZrK2QgAWAW9vog","toolName":"probe","args":{"question":"Near some point, a small change $\\Delta x$ in $x$ produces a change $\\Delta u \\approx 3\\,\\Delta x$ in $u$, and a small change $\\Delta u$ produces a change $\\Delta y \\approx 5\\,\\Delta u$ in $y$. Which statement best explains why $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ is a **product** of the two derivatives?","options":[{"label":"Each nudge in $x$ is scaled by $3$ on its way to $u$, and that change in $u$ is then scaled by $5$ on its way to $y$, so the total scaling is $3\\cdot 5$","value":"a"},{"label":"The two rates act on $y$ at the same time, so the total effect of a nudge in $x$ on $y$ is $3+5$","value":"b"},{"label":"It is a notational rule: the $du$ in $\\frac{dy}{du}\\cdot\\frac{du}{dx}$ cancels like a fraction, which is why we multiply","value":"c"},{"label":"Multiplying is a convention chosen so that the formula agrees with the power rule","value":"d"}],"explanation":"The chain is sequential: $x$ affects $y$ only *through* $u$. A nudge $\\Delta x$ becomes $\\Delta u \\approx 3\\Delta x$, and that becomes $\\Delta y \\approx 5\\Delta u \\approx 5\\cdot 3\\,\\Delta x$. Scaling factors applied one after another multiply:\n$$\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} \\approx \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}\\cdot\\frac{\\Delta u}{\\Delta x} = 5\\cdot 3 = 15$$\nThe \"cancelling $du$\" picture is a useful mnemonic that reflects this, but $\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not literally a fraction — the real reason is the sequential scaling of small changes. Adding would be right only if $x$ affected $y$ along two separate parallel routes.","correctAnswer":"a"}}

